Yusupov gives the comparison method a chapter in Chess Evolution 3, and Aagaard one in Calculation. The idea: when two candidate moves lead to similar positions, do not calculate each line to the end on its own. Put the two lines side by side and look for the one difference: a tempo, a check, a square that is covered in one line and free in the other.

Typical pairs:

- the same two moves in a different order;
- two captures with different pieces;
- two squares for the king in a pawn ending;
- two rooks for one file.

The method saves time and catches errors. Most bad move orders come from calculating the first line, seeing that it works, and assuming the second is the same.

The test is from Kasparov–Topalov, Wijk aan Zee 1999. Black has just taken the rook: 24...cxd4. White has two forcing moves with the same two pieces, 25.Qxd4+ and 25.Re7+. Same pieces, other order. Find the difference.

Bishop or knight? Yusupov devotes two chapters of Boost Your Chess 3 to it, and the answer always comes from the pawns.

The bishop is better when:

- pawns stand on both wings, so a long-range piece can attack on one side and defend on the other;
- the position is open, or can be opened;
- the enemy pawns are fixed on the bishop's colour.

The knight is better when:

- the pawns are blocked and stand on one wing;
- it has an outpost that no pawn can attack;
- the enemy bishop is bad, shut in by its own pawns.

One more point decides many endings. A knight needs an even number of moves to come back to a square, so it cannot lose a tempo. A bishop can usually make a waiting move on the same diagonal. In zugzwang fights the bishop's side has the spare moves.

Fischer–Taimanov, Candidates 1971, game 4, is the model. Pawns on both wings, Black's pawns partly on light squares, and Fischer's light-squared bishop gains tempi until the knight and king run out of moves.

A fortress is a position the stronger side cannot win however long he plays, because he cannot break in. Yusupov's chapter in Chess Evolution 3 and de la Villa's appendix list the common types:

- the wrong rook pawn: bishop and rook pawn against a king in the corner, when the bishop does not control the promotion square;
- opposite-coloured bishops with the pawns blocked on the defender's colour;
- a blocked pawn chain that the attacker's king cannot cross;
- material down, but every entry square covered, as in Carlsen–Caruana 2018.

The defender's method:

1. Recognise the fortress before you reach it, and steer for it, even at the cost of material.
2. Put the king where the attacker needs to go: usually the corner, or the square in front of the passed pawn.
3. Keep the pieces on squares that hold the line, and do not move pawns that are part of the wall.

The attacker's method is the reverse: change the structure before the fortress is complete.
